Z||000000||Leadfoot||06-06-2001||04:47 PM||||I must say this is a great forum.  I have been an avid reader since I bought my '85 SE 2.8L fiero from a friend in January.  Since then I have been resurrecting it from the dead (i.e. scrap yard) and having a blast doing it.  I am in the process of assembling my rebuilt engine but noticed that the rubber material on the harmonic balancer is bulging out on one point.  Should this be of concern?  Thanks!||||reg||5
Z||000001||88formula||06-06-2001||05:14 PM||||If the rubber is cracked or rotted than you have to replace the harmonic damper. You can get a GM unit from GM performance for $50. Since the car is so old now the damper probably needs replacing. My 50k formula needed one. A bad damper can cause bearing wear, break the crankshaft, and cause valve train wear. Go here and register, than choose a dealer to get prices and order. <A HREF="http://www.spoperformanceparts.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.spoperformanceparts.com</A> <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Dave Gunsul||06-06-2001||09:08 PM||||Since you're a student you might want to save some money and get a used one.<BR>you can get a nice used one from Twin Lakes Fiero in Paxton IL.<BR>PH# (217)-379-9140<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||3
Z||000003||Leadfoot||06-07-2001||11:07 AM||||That's just what I did.  To be on the safe side, I bought the repair sleeve just in case the one Bob at Twin Lakes gives me has a nasty groove.  Thanks!||||reg||1
